  "claims_reviewed": [
    {
      "id": "FET1",
      "block_sha256": "3a4808b57d8a301da821f80f1e83ef0236093680b1ba92b894d4dbc6ea3ee737",
      "status": "PASS",
      "scope_verdict": "PASS at the stated original scope",
      "review_note": "Isometry and density identify actC; all inverse-limit coordinates identify actT. The supplied cyclotomic congruence determines the usual integral character, without changing AI or P."
    },
    {
      "id": "FET2",
      "block_sha256": "568527c2e98c05e6a782e6321b12208de93db473e43b82068b00e8992e9186e1",
      "status": "PASS",
      "scope_verdict": "PASS at the stated original scope",
      "review_note": "The finite cyclotomic levels give Frobenius surjectivity modulo p and a nondiscrete value group. The actual closed compositum LE0 equals the completed L-cyclotomic tower, so Scholze Theorem 3.7(i) applies, including p=2 and non-Galois L."
    },
    {
      "id": "FET3",
      "block_sha256": "d2e71b3c8d627739c974d30a254d81fe5d317c9b5c6e5e1926d40365c65e17d7",
      "status": "PASS",
      "scope_verdict": "PASS at the stated original scope",
      "review_note": "The valuation intersection proves injectivity modulo p. The binomial estimate gives the sharp limit and its lift independence; recursive residue lifting gives the p-weighted expansion. Equivariance does not presuppose that g preserves E, and sharp is never treated as additive."
    },
    {
      "id": "FET4",
      "block_sha256": "71cfd0c578092c4e7a8cc4a5345d73ad36c0ab1b45210c9a18af270f0d17175a",
      "status": "PASS",
      "scope_verdict": "PASS at the stated original scope",
      "review_note": "The embedded tilt of the completed L-cyclotomic tower is fixed by G_L intersect ker chi. Fixing that tilt fixes its sharp expansions and hence L. This proves the original FaithH implication, in fact without needing its extra chi(g)=1 antecedent."
    },
    {
      "id": "FET5",
      "block_sha256": "cf963ff0543a1957a1ea403cf04a035ce86c71d56d932902561aef00340204ee",
      "status": "PASS",
      "scope_verdict": "PASS at the stated original scope",
      "review_note": "FaithH separates finite H/N cosets on S^N. The prescribed epsilon is fixed by H, epsilon-1 is a nonzero nonunit, and its powers give the infinite common-value set; the argument includes p=2."
    },
    {
      "id": "FET6",
      "block_sha256": "7b396ff0b6513fffd18ce0f5d54164d6e0d7aeac7f14f27930e4302e72620e13",
      "status": "PASS",
      "scope_verdict": "PASS at the stated original scope",
      "review_note": "Dedekind independence gives a spanning set of evaluation vectors; a determinant polynomial is nonzero at the q0 coordinate and therefore somewhere on the infinite common-value grid. Arbitrary finite index sets and zero matrix size are preserved by reindexing."
    },
    {
      "id": "FET7",
      "block_sha256": "23c075633eb7c46725726ce4277bb9ff10155ec175b1c3e9d4ccc5b4374a5f49",
      "status": "PASS",
      "scope_verdict": "PASS at the stated original scope",
      "review_note": "Teichmuller lifting preserves the chosen residue determinant and exact N-fixedness; the Witt unit criterion applies over the perfect field F. No division by p or the finite quotient order occurs."
    },
    {
      "id": "FET8",
      "block_sha256": "f0f27d81878bb755fa1a47dc41f12d648d27536c3590a0e5b632f7315f0aafe1",
      "status": "PASS",
      "scope_verdict": "PASS at the stated original scope",
      "review_note": "Coset permutation makes the trace u fixed; multiplying by its fixed inverse produces an exactly trace-one, N-fixed coefficient in the original Witt ring."
    },
    {
      "id": "FET9",
      "block_sha256": "92e624cf6302ea2f1b1134e7b8dc7d6861b4c6d5b4ed1b31d716ac2dae5d095b",
      "status": "PASS",
      "scope_verdict": "PASS at the stated original scope",
      "review_note": "Finite coset shifts give the error modulo J_m. A common smaller normal subgroup handles every summand at each later precision. Only smoothness of x and exact fixedness of the averaging coefficient are used, not continuity of arbitrary Witt coefficients."
    },
    {
      "id": "FET10",
      "block_sha256": "0a6805df14eb7903489712e4fbb1223be3f1386d38ee2174b14b1243d6fb1336",
      "status": "PASS",
      "scope_verdict": "PASS at the stated original scope",
      "review_note": "Trace-one averages improve invariance by one precision and change the preceding iterate only in J_m. Completeness supplies the limit, and stability of each J_m plus separation proves exact H-fixedness without a global continuity assumption."
    },
    {
      "id": "FET11",
      "block_sha256": "738344a4a9ed548e55543a6bba248c669aa8f0f720d7e7926a92190a7c8320e1",
      "status": "PASS",
      "scope_verdict": "PASS at the stated original scope",
      "review_note": "Finite-dimensional module topology gives continuous integral coordinates for the original lattice action. Cofinal finite Galois subgroups supply N0. The larger Zp-basis tests invertibility, while commutation with O makes the same operator an automorphism over the original tensor ring."
    },
    {
      "id": "FET12",
      "block_sha256": "3678093468d2ed75185bda03a1eb8288eec434bc38456cbe4ed0552a04a5feac",
      "status": "PASS",
      "scope_verdict": "PASS at the stated original scope",
      "review_note": "The fixed lifted vectors differ from one common automorphism image of the original O-basis by pM. The matrix 1+pZ has an integral geometric inverse, preserving lattice and rank; the empty basis handles rank zero."
    },
    {
      "id": "FET13",
      "block_sha256": "acc9d034ca21c085f451e7bb363d68abe99ab7658714e4c1d58904c23d5f8221",
      "status": "PASS",
      "scope_verdict": "PASS at the stated original scope",
      "review_note": "The literal RW, TW and FixBasis definitions agree with the construction. The finite Galois subgroup family is cofinal. No Good, crystalline or Wach assumption is inserted into the full exported fixed-basis theorem."
    },
    {
      "id": "FET14",
      "block_sha256": "753d6d448d76a40f130f32134891294d073902561f807ba118cc249deabf2067",
      "status": "PASS",
      "scope_verdict": "PASS at the stated original scope",
      "review_note": "The inherited near-parameter groups retain c(B), not all of W. Their intersection, common pole bounds, Frobenius-stable approximation, p-saturation and both summation properties apply to the same actual maps and H. Original ioE/character compatibility gives j(E) and t in the fixed ring."
    },
    {
      "id": "FET15",
      "block_sha256": "0625a7f685c349462d251d98552e06777b6e33c8822b62b97a5de7f0be102e23",
      "status": "PASS",
      "scope_verdict": "PASS at the stated original scope",
      "review_note": "The exact contraction inequality p(i+2k+1)-2k >= i+2k+2 holds for every p>=2. The fixed near-parameter sum solves the equation modulo p by telescoping; this is distinct from the subsequent p-adic limit."
    },
    {
      "id": "FET16",
      "block_sha256": "3cd935baa2f7f5b6da46a5ee8dbebc54de3f8b0c74e4b5aaae03956340e4fbb9",
      "status": "PASS",
      "scope_verdict": "PASS at the stated original scope",
      "review_note": "Choosing precision 3k0+1 before the unknown Frobenius exponent is valid because both pole bounds and error precision scale by p^N. The ordered product of Frobenius iterates has the claimed conjugacy orientation."
    },
    {
      "id": "FET17",
      "block_sha256": "0f6e7f136b3761e1a33c8c79c581e7cb39ac7565de46c7db9b694870847b210c",
      "status": "PASS",
      "scope_verdict": "PASS at the stated original scope",
      "review_note": "The negative error times the inverse has the required near-parameter order. The contraction identity gives (1+z)F1=j(A)phi(1+z) modulo p. A near-parameter geometric sum followed by a p-adic inverse makes 1+z invertible; WIS18 reflects the determinant unit to E."
    },
    {
      "id": "FET18",
      "block_sha256": "8aca3f228d53cb5e16728ea601cff47ebba767efae8932bb8d258798807573ef",
      "status": "PASS",
      "scope_verdict": "PASS at the stated original scope",
      "review_note": "IFD24 supplies surjectivity for the vectorized operator j(A)phi(z)j(A^{-1})-z. Multiplication on the right recovers the exact correction equation; IFD25 alone would not justify this existence claim."
    },
    {
      "id": "FET19",
      "block_sha256": "963fd67b04bba7e9dbd32009b6060a60233c34173576985ce3068efb0f769bf2",
      "status": "PASS",
      "scope_verdict": "PASS at the stated original scope",
      "review_note": "The maintained defect has order p^(n+1), and saturation makes its quotient fixed. The explicit correction expansion kills its residue, preserves all congruences and converges in the fixed ring and E. Separation gives the exact nonlinear equation, with V=1+pZ invertible."
    },
    {
      "id": "FET20",
      "block_sha256": "bc2674fa6a7967188cde3061aa6c4200c5c61ae0cb7e2306075e570db1d1d1d6",
      "status": "PASS",
      "scope_verdict": "PASS at the stated original scope",
      "review_note": "The three conjugacies compose in the printed order, retaining fixed and invertible matrices. Unit reflection proves the stronger invertibility of the coefficient matrix. Every original good-layer and IoW premise is retained, including zero rank and p=2."
    },
    {
      "id": "FET21",
      "block_sha256": "319a68c73454676ced77b2225ca6be5475e707c3ad36121347a1a816e34b78f5",
      "status": "PASS",
      "scope_verdict": "PASS at the stated original scope",
      "review_note": "Pure tensors show that Frobenius images span; semilinearity then makes the images of the finite basis span. Their coordinate matrix is invertible and fixed. The change e-prime=eV has exactly the stated matrix equation and ioE image."
    },
    {
      "id": "FET22",
      "block_sha256": "04ae442aa0a9bbceeaacd69a100607e2ac4d36d33c9a9d1302e86aebf9d5c474",
      "status": "PASS",
      "scope_verdict": "PASS at the stated original scope",
      "review_note": "The coordinate embedding is injective, semilinear and spanning. The already supplied fixed-column Frobenius equation gives a rectangular IFD25 descent with zero inhomogeneous term and both coefficient matrices invertible."
    },
    {
      "id": "FET23",
      "block_sha256": "2f9f3c62033e054ff4c2be80dc9a16c6fa58ef64125f8e7dc608785c3d97c7c5",
      "status": "PASS",
      "scope_verdict": "PASS at the stated original scope",
      "review_note": "Normality of H fixes the Galois-transformed basis columns; the precise gamma_E matrix yields the required Frobenius system. Character injectivity identifies the kernel of resGamma with H, and its original surjectivity gives the quotient action. All laws descend by injectivity."
    },
    {
      "id": "FET24",
      "block_sha256": "bddf1e607b08a0103892e6fe03cd583f2c694ca10df9bf9ce9e837c89db5af67",
      "status": "PASS",
      "scope_verdict": "PASS at the stated original scope",
      "review_note": "Every original equivariant O-linear lattice morphism gives a fixed column system with the source Frobenius matrix. Its unique descent proves naturality, identity, composition, addition, scalar and action compatibilities despite independently chosen bases."
    },
    {
      "id": "FET25",
      "block_sha256": "32290fc3ebf919a1f2413dd30346ca63c525e9dc9816e357e860501d0e7d4612",
      "status": "PASS",
      "scope_verdict": "PASS at the stated original scope",
      "review_note": "Every WEt record field is supplied: three module structures, both scalar towers, exact rank, O-linear semilinear Frobenius/Gamma actions, all morphism laws, and the injective spanning natural tensor comparison. No crystalline hypothesis is needed for this etale functor."
    },
    {
      "id": "FET26",
      "block_sha256": "afacec2c72d7dd01d7ac3a1b138c040071b625127d5769f92dcddf47589f24b6",
      "status": "PASS",
      "scope_verdict": "PASS at the stated original scope",
      "review_note": "WPR30 applies to this same WEt under its original Bhatt-Scholze realization and LocTied premises. Herr/class-map, quantitative comparison and common ConclF estimates are not inferred; no whole denominator obligation is closed."
    }
  ],
